var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"explanation/extensions/#How-package-extensions-load","page":"How package extensions load","text":"A package extension of the target package loads as soon as the notebook loads all its trigger packages. The extension also loads again after each click on the reload button. PlutoDevMacros handles two kinds of extensions in different ways:\n\nA direct extension is an extension of the target package itself. The [extensions] section of the Project.toml of the target package lists it.\nAn indirect extension is an extension of a dependency of the target package.\n\nThis page uses the MyPkg package and its extension MyPkgExampleExt from the tutorial Develop a package extension in a notebook.","title":"How package extensions load"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/extensions/#Direct-extensions","page":"How package extensions load","text":"Julia loads the extensions of the packages that it loaded itself. Julia did not load the MyPkg module that @fromparent makes in the notebook, so Julia does not load its extensions. PlutoDevMacros loads them instead.\n\nPlutoDevMacros adds a function to Base.package_callbacks. Julia calls the functions in this list each time it loads a package in the notebook process. The function of PlutoDevMacros then checks each extension of the target package. When Julia loaded all the trigger packages of an extension, the function evaluates the extension code. The macro does the same check at the end of each load of the target package. So when the notebook loaded the trigger packages before, the extension loads together with the target package.\n\nThe extension code is in ext/MyPkgExampleExt.jl or in ext/MyPkgExampleExt/MyPkgExampleExt.jl. PlutoDevMacros evaluates it in a new module in the same way as the code of the target package. Inside the extension code, using MyPkg points to the MyPkg module that @fromparent loaded, and using Example points to the loaded trigger package.\n\nA trigger package counts as loaded when any code in the notebook process loaded it. A plain using Example in a notebook cell is the usual case. A different package that depends on Example also loads it.\n\nEach load of the target package makes a new MyPkg module. The extensions of the previous module do not apply to it, so PlutoDevMacros loads the extensions again for the new module. This is why the tutorial shows the Loading code of extension message again after a click on the reload button.","title":"Direct extensions"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/extensions/#Indirect-extensions","page":"How package extensions load","text":"The dependencies of the target package are normal Julia packages, and Julia loads them. Julia also loads their extensions with its own mechanism. When the notebook loads the trigger packages of such an extension, Julia loads the extension. PlutoDevMacros does nothing for these extensions.\n\nA click on the reload button does not load a dependency again, so its extensions also do not load again.","title":"Indirect extensions"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/loading/#How-@frompackage-loads-the-target-package","page":"How @frompackage loads the target package","text":"Each time you run the @frompackage cell, the macro reads all the code of the target package again and evaluates it in a new module inside the notebook. Then it changes the import statements of the cell so that they import from this new module. The reload button runs the cell again, so each click gives a new module that contains the current code of the package.\n\nThis page uses @fromparent and the MyPkg package from the tutorials. @fromparent is the short form of @frompackage, and both load the target package in the same way.","title":"How @frompackage loads the target package"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/loading/#One-load-of-the-target-package","page":"How @frompackage loads the target package","text":"The macro starts from the target path and looks for the first Project.toml file in that folder or in a folder above it. That project is the target package. The macro reads its manifest, and the manifest setting can instantiate or resolve the environment first.\n\nThe macro then makes a new module MyPkg inside a new temporary module in the notebook workspace. It loads the direct dependencies of the target package with the normal package loader of Julia. After that, it reads src/MyPkg.jl, parses it, and evaluates it one top-level expression at a time. It follows each include call in the same way. When the code is evaluated, the macro calls __init__ if the package defines it, and loads the package extensions whose trigger packages are already loaded.\n\nLast, the macro changes the statements of the cell. For example, import * becomes an import statement with an explicit list of the names of the new MyPkg module. The cell output also contains the reload button.","title":"One load of the target package"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/loading/#Why-each-load-makes-a-new-module","page":"How @frompackage loads the target package","text":"On Julia 1.10 and 1.11, a module cannot get a new definition of a struct that it defines already. A new module has no old definition, so a new definition of Point works without a restart of the notebook on all Julia versions. Constants and global variables also start again from the values in the code.\n\nThe names that the @fromparent cell imports now point to the new module. Pluto knows that the cell defines these names, so it runs again all the cells that use them. The old module stays in memory, but no cell uses it.\n\nThe cell outputs show the short name, MyPkg.Point, and not the full path of the module inside the notebook workspace.","title":"Why each load makes a new module"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/loading/#The-reload-button","page":"How @frompackage loads the target package","text":"The macro reads the files of the target package only when you run its cell. A change to a file does not run the cell, so the notebook does not see the change until the next run. The reload button runs the @fromparent cell, in the same way as the run button of the cell.\n\nThe button stays visible when the load fails, so you can fix the code and load again.","title":"The reload button"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/loading/#LOAD_PATH-and-dependencies","page":"How @frompackage loads the target package","text":"The macro adds the Project.toml file of the target package to the end of LOAD_PATH. With this entry, Julia finds the dependencies of the target package in its manifest, also the dependencies that the notebook environment does not contain. The notebook environment stays first in LOAD_PATH, so the Pluto package manager keeps control of the packages that the notebook installs.\n\nThe dependencies are normal Julia packages. Julia loads each of them once for each Julia process. The reload button does not load them again. This is also true for a dependency that you added with Pkg.develop from a local path. A change to the code of such a dependency is visible only after a restart of the notebook.\n\nInside the code of the target package, an import or using statement that starts with the name of the package, such as using MyPkg.Shapes, becomes a relative import. The name MyPkg is not a package that Julia loaded, so the macro points these statements to the new module.","title":"LOAD_PATH and dependencies"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/loading/#Notebooks-that-the-target-package-includes","page":"How @frompackage loads the target package","text":"The target package can include a notebook file as source code. When that notebook calls @fromparent, the macro stops the load at the include call of the notebook file. The notebook then sees the package as it is at that line, and the notebook can import from the module that includes it with ParentModule. The code of the notebook itself runs in the cells, so it does not run two times.","title":"Notebooks that the target package includes"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/loading/#Behavior-outside-Pluto","page":"How @frompackage loads the target package","text":"Outside of a notebook, the macro does not load anything. It keeps the relative imports without * and the >. imports of direct dependencies, as plain import and using statements. It removes all the other statements. So a notebook that the package includes works as package code: @fromparent import >.Dates becomes import Dates, and @fromparent import * does nothing.\n\nThe macro does the same when it finds a @fromparent call in the code of the target package during a load from a different notebook.","title":"Behavior outside Pluto"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/loading/#Comparison-with-Revise","page":"How @frompackage loads the target package","text":"Revise also updates the code of a package in a running Julia session. It tracks the source files and changes only the methods that changed. The two tools work in different ways:\n\n@fromparent evaluates all the code again at each reload. Revise changes only the parts that changed, so it is faster for a large package.\n@fromparent makes a new module, so it can change a struct on all the Julia versions that PlutoDevMacros supports. Revise on Julia 1.10 and 1.11 cannot change a struct definition.\nA click on the reload button makes Pluto run all the cells that use the package. Pluto does not know when Revise changes a method, so the cells that use it do not run again.\n@fromparent loads the target package without a change to the notebook environment. To load a local package with using, you must turn off the Pluto package manager, for example with Pkg.activate in a cell.\nThe code of the target package is not precompiled. Each function compiles when the notebook calls it the first time after a reload.","title":"Comparison with Revise"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/root_module/#Load-the-target-package-as-a-root-module","page":"Load as a root module","text":"Use rootmodule = true when the code of the target package needs pkgdir, pathof, or pkgversion of its own module. For example, a package that reads data files from pkgdir(@__MODULE__) needs this setting.\n\nwarning: Warning\nThis setting uses Julia internals. A new Julia version can break it.","title":"Load the target package as a root module"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/root_module/#Why-the-default-fails","page":"Load as a root module","text":"@fromparent evaluates the code of the target package in a module inside the notebook. Julia does not know this module as a package, so these functions return nothing:\n\npkgdir(MyPkg), pathof(MyPkg), pkgversion(MyPkg)","title":"Why the default fails"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/root_module/#Register-the-module","page":"Load as a root module","text":"Add rootmodule = true to the @fromparent cell:\n\n@fromparent import * rootmodule = true\n\nThe macro registers the loaded module as the root module of the target package. The same three functions now return the folder of the package, the path of src/MyPkg.jl, and the version in Project.toml.\n\nWith verbose = true, each reload also shows a Replacing module log message. Julia shows it when the new module replaces the previous one.\n\nThe @frompackage reference describes all the settings.","title":"Register the module"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first_package/#Develop-your-first-package-in-a-notebook","page":"Develop your first package in a notebook","text":"In this tutorial, you make a small package called MyPkg and load it into a Pluto notebook with @fromparent. Then you change a function and a struct of the package, and you see the changes in the notebook without a restart. At the end, you add a package that only the notebook uses.\n\n<!-- TODO: add the GIF of the reload button here (docs/src/assets/reload_button.gif). -->\n\nYou need Julia 1.10 or later, and Pluto in your global environment. The Pluto installation guide shows how to install Pluto.\n\nThe finished package is in the docs/examples/MyPkg folder on GitHub. You can also download the finished notebook, first_steps.jl.","title":"Develop your first package in a notebook"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first_package/#Make-the-package","page":"Develop your first package in a notebook","text":"Open the Julia REPL in a folder of your choice. Run this code to make the package and write its manifest file:\n\nimport Pkg\nPkg.generate(\"MyPkg\")\nPkg.activate(\"MyPkg\")\nPkg.instantiate()\nPkg.activate()\n\nThe folder MyPkg now contains Project.toml, Manifest.toml, and src/MyPkg.jl.\n\nReplace all the text in MyPkg/src/MyPkg.jl with this code:\n\nmodule MyPkg\n\nexport Point, greet, distance\n\nstruct Point\n    x::Float64\n    y::Float64\nend\n\ngreet(name) = \"Hello, $(name)!\"\n\ndistance(p::Point) = sqrt(p.x^2 + p.y^2)\n\nend # module MyPkg","title":"Make the package"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first_package/#Put-a-notebook-in-the-package","page":"Develop your first package in a notebook","text":"Make a folder notebooks in the MyPkg folder. Then start Pluto from the Julia REPL:\n\nimport Pluto\nPluto.run()\n\nPluto opens in your browser. Create a new notebook. Use the file path box at the top of the notebook to save it as MyPkg/notebooks/first_steps.jl. Write the full path of the file.\n\nThe top of the notebook shows the new file name, first_steps.jl.","title":"Put a notebook in the package"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first_package/#Load-the-package","page":"Develop your first package in a notebook","text":"Type this code in the first cell of the notebook and run the cell:\n\nusing PlutoDevMacros\n\nPluto installs PlutoDevMacros in the environment of the notebook. This can take some time.\n\nAdd a cell with this code and run it:\n\n@fromparent import *\n\nA button with the text Reload MyPkg appears in the top-right corner of the notebook. @fromparent found MyPkg from the location of the notebook file, loaded it, and imported all its names.\n\nAdd a cell that calls a function of MyPkg:\n\ngreet(\"Pluto\")\n\nThe cell shows \"Hello, Pluto!\".","title":"Load the package"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first_package/#Change-a-function","page":"Develop your first package in a notebook","text":"In MyPkg/src/MyPkg.jl, change the greet function to this code, and save the file:\n\ngreet(name) = \"Hi, $(name)!\"\n\nClick Reload MyPkg. The greet(\"Pluto\") cell runs again and shows \"Hi, Pluto!\".","title":"Change a function"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first_package/#Change-a-struct","page":"Develop your first package in a notebook","text":"Add two cells to the notebook:\n\np = Point(3, 4)\n\ndistance(p)\n\nThe first cell shows the fields of p. The second cell shows 5.0.\n\nNow give Point a third field. In MyPkg/src/MyPkg.jl, replace the Point struct and the distance function with this code, and save the file:\n\nstruct Point\n    x::Float64\n    y::Float64\n    z::Float64\nend\n\ndistance(p::Point) = sqrt(p.x^2 + p.y^2 + p.z^2)\n\nClick Reload MyPkg. The p = Point(3, 4) cell shows a MethodError, because Point now needs three numbers. Change the cell to this code and run it:\n\np = Point(2, 3, 6)\n\nThe distance(p) cell shows 7.0. You changed a struct without a restart of the notebook. The page How @frompackage loads the target package explains why this works.","title":"Change a struct"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first_package/#Add-a-package-that-only-the-notebook-uses","page":"Develop your first package in a notebook","text":"Add a cell with this code and run it:\n\nusing PlutoUI\n\nThe Pluto package manager installs PlutoUI in the environment of the notebook. The MyPkg/Project.toml file does not change, so PlutoUI is not a dependency of MyPkg.\n\nAdd a cell with a text box:\n\n@bind name TextField(default = \"Pluto\")\n\nThe cell shows a text box that contains Pluto. Change the greet(\"Pluto\") cell to this code and run it:\n\ngreet(name)\n\nType a different name in the text box. The greet(name) cell shows the new name.","title":"Add a package that only the notebook uses"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first_package/#Next-steps","page":"Develop your first package in a notebook","text":"You made a package, loaded it in a notebook, and changed its code with the notebook open. To continue:\n\nAdd a package extension to MyPkg in the next tutorial, Develop a package extension in a notebook.\nRead the @frompackage reference for the import syntax and the settings.","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/instantiate_environment/#Instantiate-the-environment-of-the-target-package","page":"Instantiate the environment","text":"With the default settings, @fromparent uses the manifest file of the target package as it is. If the target package has no manifest, the cell shows this error:\n\nAssertionError: A manifest could not be found at the project's location.\n\nUse the manifest setting to let @fromparent fix the environment before it loads the target package. The @frompackage reference describes all the settings.","title":"Instantiate the environment of the target package"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/instantiate_environment/#Make-a-missing-manifest-or-install-missing-packages","page":"Instantiate the environment","text":"Add manifest = :instantiate to the @fromparent cell:\n\n@fromparent import * manifest = :instantiate\n\nThe macro runs Pkg.instantiate on the environment of the target package. This makes the manifest if it does not exist and installs the packages that the manifest lists. Use it after you clone a package that does not commit its manifest.","title":"Make a missing manifest or install missing packages"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/instantiate_environment/#Update-the-manifest-after-you-change-Project.toml","page":"Instantiate the environment","text":"Add manifest = :resolve to the @fromparent cell:\n\n@fromparent import * manifest = :resolve\n\nThe macro runs Pkg.resolve on the environment of the target package. Use it after you add a dependency or change a compat entry in Project.toml by hand.","title":"Update the manifest after you change Project.toml"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/instantiate_environment/#Keep-reloads-fast","page":"Instantiate the environment","text":"The macro runs Pkg.instantiate or Pkg.resolve each time it loads the target package, also when you click the reload button. Remove the setting when the manifest is correct.\n\nYou can also do this step once in the Julia REPL, outside the notebook:\n\nimport Pkg\nPkg.activate(\"path/to/MyPkg\")\nPkg.instantiate()","title":"Keep reloads fast"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/instantiate_environment/#Versioned-and-workspace-manifests","page":"Instantiate the environment","text":"@fromparent finds the manifest file in the same way as Julia. It uses a versioned manifest, for example Manifest-v1.12.toml, when it matches the Julia version. When the target package is a project of a Julia workspace, it uses the manifest in the folder of the workspace.","title":"Versioned and workspace manifests"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/notebook_only_code/#Run-code-only-inside-or-outside-the-notebook","page":"Run code only inside or outside the notebook","text":"A Pluto notebook file is a Julia file, so the target package can include it as source code. Use @only_in_nb for code that must run only when you open the notebook, such as examples and checks. Use @only_out_nb for code that must run only when the package loads the file.\n\nThe example on this page adds a notebook scale.jl to the MyPkg package from the tutorials.","title":"Run code only inside or outside the notebook"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/notebook_only_code/#Add-the-dependencies","page":"Run code only inside or outside the notebook","text":"The first lines of a notebook file contain using Markdown and using InteractiveUtils. The package code also calls using PlutoDevMacros. Add these three packages to the dependencies of MyPkg. In the Julia REPL, run:\n\nimport Pkg\nPkg.activate(\"path/to/MyPkg\")\nPkg.add([\"PlutoDevMacros\", \"Markdown\", \"InteractiveUtils\"])","title":"Add the dependencies"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/notebook_only_code/#Write-the-notebook","page":"Run code only inside or outside the notebook","text":"Save a new notebook as MyPkg/src/scale.jl, with these cells:\n\nusing PlutoDevMacros\n\n@fromparent import *\n\nscale(p::Point, k) = Point(k * p.x, k * p.y, k * p.z)\n\n@only_in_nb scale(Point(1, 2, 3), 2)\n\nAdd this line to MyPkg/src/MyPkg.jl, before end # module MyPkg:\n\ninclude(\"scale.jl\")","title":"Write the notebook"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/notebook_only_code/#Know-what-runs-where","page":"Run code only inside or outside the notebook","text":"In the notebook scale.jl, @fromparent loads MyPkg up to the include(\"scale.jl\") line. The @only_in_nb cell runs and shows the scaled point.\n\nWhen MyPkg loads in any other way, the @only_in_nb cell does nothing. This is the case for using MyPkg in the Julia REPL, and for a different notebook that loads MyPkg with @fromparent. In these cases, @only_out_nb code runs, and scale is a function of MyPkg.\n\nOutside of the notebook, @fromparent keeps only some import statements. The @frompackage reference lists them.","title":"Know what runs where"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/loading_problems/#Find-loading-problems","page":"Find loading problems","text":"When the target package does not load, the @fromparent cell shows the error. The reload button stays in the top-right corner of the notebook and gets a red border. Fix the code of the package, save the file, and click the reload button.\n\nThe cells that use names from the target package show UndefVarError until the @fromparent cell runs without an error. Fix the error in the @fromparent cell first.","title":"Find loading problems"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/loading_problems/#Read-the-error","page":"Find loading problems","text":"For a syntax error in the code of the target package, the cell shows a LoadError with the file and the line of the error:\n\nLoadError: ParseError:\n# Error @ /path/to/MyPkg/src/MyPkg.jl:11:35\n\nFor other errors, the cell shows the error message and the stack trace.\n\nHold the Ctrl key and click the reload button to scroll to the @fromparent cell.","title":"Read the error"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/loading_problems/#Show-the-load-steps","page":"Find loading problems","text":"Add verbose = true to the @fromparent cell:\n\n@fromparent import * verbose = true\n\nThe cell then shows log messages about the load steps. These are some of them:\n\nAdding .../MyPkg/Project.toml to end of LOAD_PATH\nInstantiating Manifest as explicitly requested, with the manifest = :instantiate setting\nLoading code of extension MyPkgExampleExt for package MyPkg\n\nWith the manifest setting, verbose = true also shows the output of the package manager.","title":"Show the load steps"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/loading_problems/#Fix-common-errors","page":"Find loading problems","text":"This table lists errors that @fromparent shows and their usual cause.\n\nError message starts with Cause Fix\nA manifest could not be found The target package has no manifest. Instantiate the environment.\nNo project was found starting from No folder above the notebook contains a Project.toml. Save the notebook inside the folder of the target package.\n@frompackage can only be called with a Package as target The Project.toml has no name and uuid. Add them, or point the macro to the folder of a package.\nThe package with name ... could not be found as a dependency A >. statement names a package that the target package does not depend on. Add the dependency, or load the package without >..\nThe provided extra arguments at the end of the macro call are not in a supported format A setting has a wrong name or type. See the settings table in the @frompackage reference.\nMultiple definitions for Two cells define the same name, or the notebook has two @fromparent cells. Handle names that the notebook defines already.","title":"Fix common errors"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/import_names/#Import-specific-names-and-submodules","page":"Import specific names and submodules","text":"@fromparent import * imports all the names of the target package. To import fewer names, or names from a submodule or a dependency, change the import statement. The @frompackage reference lists the full import syntax.\n\nThe examples on this page use the MyPkg package from the tutorials.","title":"Import specific names and submodules"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/import_names/#Import-some-names","page":"Import specific names and submodules","text":"List the names after ^:. The ^ symbol is the top module of the target package:\n\n@fromparent import ^: greet, distance\n\nThe name of the target package also works as the start of the module path:\n\n@fromparent import MyPkg: greet, distance","title":"Import some names"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/import_names/#Import-from-a-submodule","page":"Import specific names and submodules","text":"Put the path of the submodule after ^.. For a submodule MyPkg.Shapes, this cell imports the names that Shapes exports:\n\n@fromparent using ^.Shapes\n\nThis cell imports only the module name Shapes:\n\n@fromparent import ^.Shapes\n\nAdd as to give the module a different name in the notebook:\n\n@fromparent import MyPkg.Shapes as S","title":"Import from a submodule"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/import_names/#Load-a-dependency-of-the-target-package","page":"Import specific names and submodules","text":"Put >. before the name of the dependency. The dependency can be direct or indirect:\n\n@fromparent import >.Dates\n\nThe notebook environment does not have to contain the dependency. The Pluto package manager does not add it to the notebook environment.","title":"Load a dependency of the target package"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/import_names/#Use-more-than-one-statement","page":"Import specific names and submodules","text":"Use one @fromparent cell for each notebook. Two cells with @fromparent give a Multiple definitions error. Put all the statements in a begin ... end block, and put the settings after end:\n\n@fromparent begin\n    import ^: greet, distance\n    using ^.Shapes\n    import >.Dates\nend verbose = true","title":"Use more than one statement"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/import_names/#Leave-out-the-names-from-using-statements","page":"Import specific names and submodules","text":"import * also imports the names that using statements in the target package bring into scope. For example, using Dates in MyPkg makes import * import today, Date, and the other exported names of Dates. Put @exclude_using before the statement to import only the names that the package defines or imports itself:\n\n@fromparent @exclude_using import *","title":"Leave out the names from using statements"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/import_names/#Handle-names-that-the-notebook-defines-already","page":"Import specific names and submodules","text":"A statement without a list of names, such as import * or using ^.Shapes, skips the names that a notebook cell defines already. The macro does not show a warning for these names. To get the version from the package, rename the variable in the notebook.\n\nA statement with a list of names imports all the names in the list. If a notebook cell also defines one of these names, Pluto shows a Multiple definitions error.","title":"Handle names that the notebook defines already"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/other_exports/#Other-exports","page":"Other exports","text":"This page lists the other macros and functions of PlutoDevMacros. The package exports all of them except PlutoDevMacros.hide_this_log.","title":"Other exports"},{"category":"macro","location":"reference/other_exports/#PlutoDevMacros.@addmethod","page":"Other exports","text":"@addmethod func(args...;kwargs...) = body\n@addmethod function func(args...;kwargs...) \n\tbody\nend\n\nThis simple macro modifies a function definition expression (only when called from a Pluto notebook) to prepend the name of the module defining the function (here called DefiningModule) to the method definition.\n\nSo the code\n\n@addmethod func(args...;kwargs...) = something\n\nis simply translated to\n\nDefiningModule.func(args...;kwargs...) = something\n\nwhen called from a Pluto notebook, and to:\n\nfunc(args...;kwargs...) = something\n\nwhen called outside of Pluto.\n\nThis is useful to avoid multiple definition errors inside Pluto but has the caveat that defining a method with @addmethod does not trigger a reactive run of all cells that call the modified function. This also mean that when removing the cell with the @addmethod call, the actual method added to the DefiningModule will not be automatically erased by Pluto and will still accessible until it is not overwritten with another method with the same signature. \n\nThis is easy to fix in the case of adding methods to modules loaded with @frompackage/@fromparent as reloading the module is sufficient to remove the hanging method.\n\nSee this video for an example:\n\n(Image: Link to Video)\n\nSee also: @frompackage, @fromparent\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlutoDevMacros.@addmethod"},{"category":"macro","location":"reference/other_exports/#PlutoDevMacros.@only_in_nb","page":"Other exports","text":"@only_in_nb ex\n\nExecutes the expression ex only if the macro is called from a running Pluto instance and ran directly from the source notebook file.\n\nThis is more strict than PlutoHooks.@skip_as_script as including a notebook with @skip_as_script ex from another notebook would still execute ex.\n@only_in_nb ex instead only evaluates ex if the calling notebook is the original source notebook file.\n\nSee also: @only_out_nb, PlutoHooks.@skip_as_script.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlutoDevMacros.@only_in_nb"},{"category":"macro","location":"reference/other_exports/#PlutoDevMacros.@only_out_nb","page":"Other exports","text":"@only_out_nb ex\n\nOpposite of @only_in_nb\n\nSee also: @only_in_nb, PlutoHooks.@only_as_script.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlutoDevMacros.@only_out_nb"},{"category":"macro","location":"reference/other_exports/#PlutoDevMacros.@current_pluto_cell_id","page":"Other exports","text":"@current_pluto_cell_id()\n\nReturns the cell_id (as a string) of the cell where the macro is called. If not ran from within the pluto notebook containing the call, returns an empty string\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlutoDevMacros.@current_pluto_cell_id"},{"category":"macro","location":"reference/other_exports/#PlutoDevMacros.@current_pluto_notebook_file","page":"Other exports","text":"@current_pluto_notebook_file()\n\nReturns the path of the notebook file of the cell where the macro is called. If not ran from within the pluto notebook containing the call, returns an empty string\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlutoDevMacros.@current_pluto_notebook_file"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/other_exports/#PlutoDevMacros.plutodump","page":"Other exports","text":"plutodump(x::Union{Symbol, Expr}; maxdepth = 8)\n\nDumps a symbol or expression directly as text on the pluto cell output instead of in the stdout\n\nSee also: Meta.dump\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlutoDevMacros.plutodump"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/other_exports/#PlutoDevMacros.hide_this_log","page":"Other exports","text":"hide_this_log(html_content::AbstractString = \"\")\nhide_this_log(html::Docs.HTML)\n\nSimple function that returns a Docs.HTML object which when sent to Pluto logs with e.g. @info (or any other loggin macro), will hide the log from view.\n\nIt is mostly intended to send some javascript to the logs for execution but avoid having an empty log box hanging around below the cell. The output of this function contains a script which will hide the specific log that contains it, and if no other logs are present, will also hide the log container in Pluto.\n\nThe function also optionally accept some content that will be inserted just before the script for hiding the log. The custom input can be provided as an AbstractString, or directly as an HTML object of type Docs.HTML, in which case it will simply extract the HTML contents as String from the contents field. The provided content will be directly interpreted as HTML, meaning that any script will have to be surrounded by <script> tags.\n\nThis function is used inside PlutoDevMacros to create the reload @fromparent button also via logs for redundancy.\n\nExample\n\nSuppose you are inside a Pluto notebook and you want to execute some custom javascript from a cell, but for some reason you don't want to have the javascript to be the final output of your cell. You can exploit logs for this (assuming you didn't disable logging for the cell in question).\n\nThe following snippet can be inserted in a cell to send a custom message on the javascript developer console while still having a non-javascript cell output.\n\njulia_output = let\n    @info PlutoDevMacros.hide_this_log(html\"<script>console.log('message')</script>\")\n    3\nend\n\nWhich will correctly send the message to the console even if the cell output is not the javascript script: (Image: hide_this_log example gif)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlutoDevMacros.hide_this_log"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/frompackage/#@frompackage-and-@fromparent","page":"@frompackage and @fromparent","text":"","title":"@frompackage and @fromparent"},{"category":"macro","location":"reference/frompackage/#PlutoDevMacros.FromPackage.@frompackage","page":"@frompackage and @fromparent","text":"@frompackage target import_block [option = value ...]\n\nLoad the target package into the Pluto notebook and run the import statements of import_block against it.\n\nThe target package is the package of the first Project.toml in the folder of target or in one of its parent folders. target is a path to a file or a folder. A relative path starts from the folder of the notebook file. Outside of a notebook, target must be a String or a raw\"...\" string. Inside a notebook, target can be any expression that returns a path, for example @__FILE__.\n\nEach call parses and evaluates the code of the target package again, as a submodule of the notebook workspace. The macro also shows a reload button in the top-right corner of the notebook. Click it to load the target package again after you change its code.\n\nUse @fromparent when the notebook file is inside the folder of the target package. @fromparent import_block is the short form of @frompackage @__FILE__ import_block.\n\nThis cell loads the package in the folder path/to/MyPkg, imports all its names, and loads LocalDependency, a dependency of MyPkg:\n\n@frompackage \"path/to/MyPkg\" begin\n    import ^: *\n    using >.LocalDependency\nend\n\nLocalDependency does not have to be in the notebook environment.\n\nImport syntax\n\nimport_block is one import or using statement, or a begin ... end block of statements. The first name of the module path selects the source module:\n\nSyntax Source Example\nPackageModule or ^ The top module of the target package. import ^: func\nThe name of the target package The top module of the target package. using MyPkg.SubModule\nParentModule or < The module that includes the target file. import <: func\n. (relative import) A path relative to the module that includes the target file. import ..Sibling: func\n>. A dependency of the target package, direct or indirect. using >.JSON\n* (catch-all) ParentModule if the target package includes the target file, otherwise PackageModule. import *\n\nSome rules apply to these statements:\n\nParentModule, <, and relative imports work only when the target package includes the target file.\nA catch-all import import Module: * imports all the names that Module defines or imports. It also imports the names that using statements inside Module bring into scope. You cannot use * with >. or with a list of other names.\nPut @exclude_using before a catch-all import to leave out the names from using statements, for example @exclude_using import *.\nA statement without a list of names (import *, using ^.SubModule, or import ^.SubModule) does not import a name that the notebook defines already. The macro skips these names and does not show a warning. A statement with a list of names, as import ^: func, imports all the names in the list.\nOne statement can contain more than one module, for example using >.JSON, >.Markdown.\n\nOutside of Pluto, the macro keeps only relative imports without * and >. imports of direct dependencies. It removes all other statements. For example, import >.JSON becomes import JSON when JSON is a direct dependency of the target package.\n\nSettings\n\nPut settings after import_block in the form name = value:\n\n@fromparent import * verbose = true manifest = :instantiate\n\nSetting Default Effect\nmanifest::Symbol :none :none uses the manifest in the environment of the target package and gives an error if there is no manifest. :resolve runs Pkg.resolve on that environment first. :instantiate runs Pkg.instantiate on it first.\nrootmodule::Bool false true registers the loaded module as a root module, so it behaves more like a module loaded with using MyPkg. This setting uses Julia internals.\nverbose::Bool false true shows log messages about the load steps, for example changes to LOAD_PATH and loaded extensions.\n\nSee also: @fromparent\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlutoDevMacros.FromPackage.@frompackage"},{"category":"macro","location":"reference/frompackage/#PlutoDevMacros.FromPackage.@fromparent","page":"@frompackage and @fromparent","text":"@fromparent import_block [option = value ...]\n\nShort form of @frompackage that uses the calling file as target. These two calls are equal:\n\n@fromparent import_block\n@frompackage @__FILE__ import_block\n\nThe target package is the package that contains the notebook file. The @frompackage docstring describes the import syntax and the settings.\n\nSee also: @addmethod\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlutoDevMacros.FromPackage.@fromparent"},{"category":"section","location":"","page":"Home","text":"<div id=\"landing\" class=\"landing\">\n<header class=\"landing-hero\">\n  <div class=\"landing-hero__text\">\n    <p class=\"landing-name\" style=\"--landing-name-chars: 17\">PlutoDevMacros.jl</p>\n    <h1 class=\"landing-title\">Develop a Julia package from a Pluto notebook</h1>\n    <p class=\"landing-tagline\">Load the code of your package into a notebook, change it, and click reload to test the change.</p>\n    <div class=\"landing-actions\">\n<a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--brand\" href=\"tutorials/first_package/\">First tutorial</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"reference/frompackage/\">Reference</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"https://github.com/disberd/PlutoDevMacros.jl\">View on GitHub</a>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</header>\n<section class=\"landing-features\" aria-label=\"Features\">\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"tutorials/first_package/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">🔄</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Reload button</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\"><code>@fromparent</code> loads the target package and shows a button that loads it again after you change the code.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"reference/frompackage/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">📦</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Import syntax</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">Import all names with <code>import *</code>, or import specific names, submodules, and dependencies.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"tutorials/package_extension/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">🧩</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Package extensions</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">The extensions of the target package load when the notebook loads their trigger packages.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"reference/frompackage/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">⚙️</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Settings</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">Resolve or instantiate the environment of the target package, and show log messages about each load step.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"howto/notebook_only_code/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">📓</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Notebook-only code</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\"><code>@only_in_nb</code> and <code>@only_out_nb</code> run code only inside or only outside the notebook.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"howto/addmethod/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">➕</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Add methods</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\"><code>@addmethod</code> adds methods to functions of the target package from a notebook cell.</p>\n  </a>\n</section>\n</div>","title":"Home"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quickstart","page":"Home","text":"Put a Pluto notebook in the folder of your package and run this code in a cell:\n\nusing PlutoDevMacros\n\n@fromparent import *\n\nThe cell loads your package and imports all its names into the notebook. Click the reload button in the top-right corner of the notebook after you change the code of the package.\n\n<!-- TODO: add the GIF of the reload button here (docs/src/assets/reload_button.gif). -->","title":"Quickstart"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/addmethod/#Add-methods-to-functions-of-the-target-package","page":"Add methods from the notebook","text":"To add a method to a function of the target package from a notebook cell, put @addmethod before the method definition. With the MyPkg package from the tutorials:\n\n@addmethod greet(x::Int) = \"number $x\"\n\ngreet(3)\n\nThe second cell shows \"number 3\". The function form also works:\n\n@addmethod function greet(x::Int)\n    return \"number $x\"\nend\n\nWithout @addmethod, the cell greet(x::Int) = \"number $x\" defines a new greet in the notebook. Pluto then shows a Multiple definitions for greet error, because the @fromparent cell also defines greet.","title":"Add methods to functions of the target package"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/addmethod/#Know-when-cells-run-again","page":"Add methods from the notebook","text":"The cells that call greet do not run again when the @addmethod cell runs. Run them yourself.\nA click on the reload button runs the @addmethod cell again, because the cell uses greet. The method is then part of the new module.\nIf you delete the @addmethod cell, the method stays until the next reload.","title":"Know when cells run again"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/addmethod/#Use-it-in-a-notebook-that-the-package-includes","page":"Add methods from the notebook","text":"Outside of the notebook, @addmethod does not change the definition. In a notebook that the target package includes, the definition then adds a normal method when the package loads. The page Run code only inside or outside the notebook shows how a package includes a notebook.","title":"Use it in a notebook that the package includes"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/notebook_only_packages/#Use-packages-that-only-the-notebook-needs","page":"Use packages that only the notebook needs","text":"Load the package in a notebook cell with using or import:\n\nusing BenchmarkTools\n\nThe Pluto package manager installs the package in the environment of the notebook. The Project.toml file of the target package does not change. Use these packages to plot, benchmark, or make interactive controls for the code of the target package. For example, with the MyPkg package from the tutorials:\n\n@benchmark distance($(Point(2, 3, 6)))","title":"Use packages that only the notebook needs"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/notebook_only_packages/#Use-a-dependency-of-the-target-package","page":"Use packages that only the notebook needs","text":"To use a package that the target package depends on, load it with >. in the @fromparent cell:\n\n@fromparent begin\n    import *\n    import >.Dates\nend\n\nThe notebook then uses the same package as the target package. The Pluto package manager does not add it to the notebook environment.","title":"Use a dependency of the target package"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/notebook_only_packages/#Know-which-version-loads","page":"Use packages that only the notebook needs","text":"@fromparent adds the Project.toml file of the target package to the end of LOAD_PATH. Julia looks for a package in the environments of LOAD_PATH in order, so it looks in the notebook environment first. When the notebook environment and the target package both contain a package, Julia loads the version from the notebook environment.\n\nDo not load a dependency of the target package with a plain using in the notebook if the version must match the manifest of the target package. Use >. for it.\n\nThe page How @frompackage loads the target package explains the role of LOAD_PATH.","title":"Know which version loads"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/package_extension/#Develop-a-package-extension-in-a-notebook","page":"Develop a package extension in a notebook","text":"In this tutorial, you add a package extension to MyPkg and develop it in a Pluto notebook. The extension loads when the notebook loads its trigger package. Then you change the extension and load it again with the reload button.\n\nDo the first tutorial, Develop your first package in a notebook, before this one. This tutorial starts from the MyPkg package that you made there.\n\nThe trigger package is Example.jl, a small registered package. Its function hello(who) returns \"Hello, $who\".\n\nThe finished package is in the docs/examples/MyPkg folder on GitHub. You can also download the finished notebook, extension.jl.","title":"Develop a package extension in a notebook"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/package_extension/#Add-the-extension-to-the-package","page":"Develop a package extension in a notebook","text":"Add these lines to the end of MyPkg/Project.toml:\n\n[weakdeps]\nExample = \"7876af07-990d-54b4-ab0e-23690620f79a\"\n\n[extensions]\nMyPkgExampleExt = \"Example\"\n\nIn MyPkg/src/MyPkg.jl, add shout to the export line. Then add a function without methods before end # module MyPkg:\n\nexport Point, greet, distance, shout\n\n# The methods of this function come from the MyPkgExampleExt extension.\nfunction shout end\n\nMake a folder ext in the MyPkg folder. In that folder, make the file MyPkgExampleExt.jl with this code:\n\nmodule MyPkgExampleExt\n\nusing MyPkg\nusing Example\n\nMyPkg.shout(name) = uppercase(Example.hello(name))\n\nend # module MyPkgExampleExt\n\nThe MyPkg folder now contains the folders ext, notebooks, and src.","title":"Add the extension to the package"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/package_extension/#Load-the-package-in-a-new-notebook","page":"Develop a package extension in a notebook","text":"Start Pluto and create a new notebook. Save it as MyPkg/notebooks/extension.jl. Add these two cells and run them:\n\nusing PlutoDevMacros\n\n@fromparent import * verbose = true\n\nThe Reload MyPkg button appears in the top-right corner of the notebook. The verbose = true setting makes @fromparent show log messages about the load steps. The second cell shows a message that starts with Adding and ends with to end of LOAD_PATH.","title":"Load the package in a new notebook"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/package_extension/#Load-the-trigger-package","page":"Develop a package extension in a notebook","text":"Add a cell that loads Example, and run it:\n\nusing Example\n\nPluto installs Example in the environment of the notebook. The cell shows this log message:\n\nLoading code of extension MyPkgExampleExt for package MyPkg\n\nThe extension loaded when the notebook loaded Example. The page How package extensions load explains how this works.\n\nAdd a cell that calls the new method:\n\nshout(\"Pluto\")\n\nThe cell shows \"HELLO, PLUTO\".","title":"Load the trigger package"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/package_extension/#Change-the-extension","page":"Develop a package extension in a notebook","text":"In MyPkg/ext/MyPkgExampleExt.jl, change the shout method to this code, and save the file:\n\nMyPkg.shout(name) = uppercase(Example.hello(name)) * \"!\"\n\nClick Reload MyPkg. The @fromparent cell shows the Loading code of extension MyPkgExampleExt for package MyPkg message again. The shout(\"Pluto\") cell shows \"HELLO, PLUTO!\".","title":"Change the extension"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/package_extension/#Next-steps","page":"Develop a package extension in a notebook","text":"You added an extension to MyPkg, loaded it from a notebook, and changed it with the notebook open. To continue, read the @frompackage reference for all the settings, including verbose.","title":"Next steps"}]
}
